# test/faults.rec - the declared FAULT SPACE, and what each one must produce.
#
# A fault suite with no denominator is a pile of anecdotes: you poke at the
# machine, nothing catches fire, and "it did not crash" quietly becomes the
# bar. This file is the denominator. Every fault the VM can inject is declared
# here WITH ITS EXPECTED RESPONSE, whether or not anything exercises it yet, so
# the gap between "faults we know about" and "faults we check" is visible
# instead of imagined. Same move as test/mutants.rec for guards, and
# THEORY.md for invariants.
#
# THE EXPECTED RESPONSE IS THE POINT. "It survived" is not a result; a fault
# that produces NO response is a FINDING, because every one of these is
# something an operator would need to be told about. The responses are drawn
# from the vocabulary the package already has:
#
#   ACT      the edge still happens, the lock is never suppressed
#   ALERT    a human is told (hook-failed, drift, overdue)
#   DEGRADE  the tier reports n/a or declines, and says so
#   RECOVER  the ladder returns to a coherent rung on its own
#   REFUSE   the request is declined, loudly, and nothing changes
#
# INJECT THE REAL FAULT, NOT ITS SIMULATION. The probe knobs
# (VIGILANCE_LOCKER_UP, VIGILANCE_SYS_BACKLIGHT, ...) exist so the STUB tier
# can model a device it does not have. Using them here would measure a fixture
# again, which is exactly how `activating` shipped. In a guest we can kill the
# real process, mount over the real directory and cut the real power.
#
# THAT RULE IS ABOUT THE MACHINE, NOT ABOUT EXIT CODES. A hook that exits 78 IS
# the fault: there is no device being pretended at, so a cell whose fault is
# purely a hook's exit status is honestly covered in the stub tier, and saying
# so beats writing a slower duplicate to look thorough. What may never be faked
# is the MACHINE: a device, a process, a filesystem, a clock.
#
# AND SOME FAULTS CANNOT BE INJECTED AS ROOT AT ALL. `chmod a-w` does nothing
# to root, so "unwritable" needs a read-only MOUNT; the same reason
# test/actuators declares `require unprivileged`. A fault that did not take
# makes every assertion after it pass for the wrong reason, so each cell
# asserts its own precondition.
#
# Format, one record per fault:
#
#   = <id>                a short name, unique
#   m <mechanism>         what is being faulted
#   f <fault>             what is DONE to it, in the guest
#   e <response>          what must happen, from the vocabulary above
#   t <test>|-            the scenario that exercises it, or `-` for not yet

= locker-killed-while-locked
m the lock provider
f SIGKILL swaylock while the machine is at the lock rung
e RECOVER: ExecStopPost crosses unlock, depth returns to open
t fault-locker

# THE WORSE HALF OF THE SAME MECHANISM. Above, a locker that was up dies and
# must be RECOVERED from; here one never comes up and must be REPORTED, or
# `go lock` returns success about a session nothing is guarding and
# lock-on-sleep lets the box suspend on the strength of it.
#
# VIGILANCE_LOCKER is a SHIPPED KNOB, not a probe override: the locker is the
# integrator's choice, so supplying one that exits non-zero is a real locker
# behaving badly. It fakes no device, process, filesystem or clock, which is the
# line this file draws.
= locker-dies-before-committing
m the lock provider
f a locker that exits non-zero before taking the session lock
e ALERT: the provider fails loudly and verify lock FAILS
t fault-locker

= hook-hangs-before-the-provider
m the act tier
f a lock.d hook ordered ahead of the provider that sleeps forever
e ACT: the per-hook bound fires and the lock STILL happens
t fault-act

= hook-fails
m the act tier
f a lock.d hook that exits 1
e ALERT: hook-failed raised, and the edge still crosses
t fault-act

= every-verify-hook-declines
m the verify tier
f all wired verify hooks exit 78
e DEGRADE: "NOTHING CHECKED" is a FAILURE, never a pass
t not-applicable

= runtime-dir-read-only
m the depth record
f a read-only tmpfs mounted over the runtime dir (root ignores modes)
e ACT: the depth write fails and THE LOCK STILL HAPPENS
t fault-state

= crossing-lock-leaked
m the crossing lock
f leave a lock file behind naming a dead process
e ACT: it is broken and the crossing proceeds
t cross-lock

# COVERED IN TWO PLACES, and only one can be the `t`. session-idle case 4 kills
# a REAL swayidle and asserts the watchdog declines; report's half of the
# response needs a graphical session to withhold its finding against, which root
# in the guest does not have, so it lives in report.t against a pinned probe.
= idle-timer-killed
m the idle timer
f SIGKILL swayidle
e ALERT: report says it is not running; the watchdog DECLINES, not accuses
t session-idle

= idle-timer-restarted-from-sleep
m the idle timer
f restart the idle unit while the machine is at the sleep rung
e DEGRADE: the ascent ceilings the clock; no false overdue
t session-idle

= two-lock-requests-at-once
m the crossing
f two `go lock` in the same instant
e ACT: one crossing, both callers succeed
t session-ladder

= lock-request-from-a-deeper-rung
m the ladder
f a Session.Lock arriving at the sleep rung
e REFUSE: decline to raise, and log it
t session-ladder

# THE ONLY CELL WITH THREE SHIPPED FIXES BEHIND IT. Each was right about the
# variant in front of it: the depth moving, a crossing already running, and a
# crossing that STARTS mid-verify. The last one is not a state the recheck can
# check for at any point, because the alert has already left the process by the
# time the state could be re-read, so the response is DEGRADE (the verdict is
# discarded and nothing is notified), and the log carries the whole finding.
= crossing-during-a-recheck
m the standing recheck
f a real `go` crossing an edge while the recheck is inside its verify tier
e DEGRADE: no verdict and NO notification; the log keeps the record
t fault-recheck

# DRIVEN BY A REAL SWITCH AT LAST. Every earlier test of this path used
# `loginctl lock-session`, which is the SIGNAL rather than the CAUSE, and the
# bug
# was about what a LID does. Three preconditions had to be found first, each of
# which silently held the whole chain open:
#
#   a LOCKABLE session   Class=greeter cannot be locked ("Session does not
#                        support lock screen"), so greetd's default slot is no
#                        use; `su -l` gives Class=user, and that one locks
#   a reachable trigger  the unit rendered for root points into /root, which
#                        the second account cannot read: it sits `activating`
#   lid policy = lock    the guest defaulted to HandleLidSwitch=suspend, so
#                        the injected lid SUSPENDED the machine under test.
#                        Very likely why this hung the guest the first time
#                        and had to be set aside.
#
# AND THE FIRST CLOSE IS NOT ACTED ON by logind (measured: four lid-closed
# events,
# two locks), so the scenario primes and ASSERTS that priming worked.
# Otherwise a
# missed event reads exactly like a passing lid-close-at-sleep.
= lid-close-at-sleep
m logind
f a real SW_LID close event via uinput, at the sleep rung
e REFUSE: the machine must NOT be raised, and the decline is logged
t fault-lid

# ASSERTED ON THE EDGE, not the resting depth. Measured: the lid crosses `lock`
# correctly and the machine is back at `open` two seconds later, because the
# provider starts a locker, the locker cannot survive a headless guest, and its
# ExecStopPost unwinds the ladder. That is locker-killed-while-locked working,
# so
# demanding a resting depth here would fail this cell over another one's
# subject.
= lid-close-at-open
m logind
f a real SW_LID close event via uinput, at the open rung
e ACT: the lock edge is crossed; the ladder descends rather than rising
t fault-lid

= suspend-mid-crossing
m the depth record
f a real S3 cycle while an edge is being crossed
e RECOVER: depth survives and the resume lands coherent
t depth-survives-suspend

# THE ONLY CELL A DESK COULD NEVER TEST, and the only one that cannot be a
# scenario: the guest is the thing being killed, so nothing inside it survives
# to report and the driver runs on the host (a `/` in the test column marks
# that). The kill is AIMED: the guest announces the instant it is inside the
# act tier with the depth committed and the crossing lock held, because a
# random kill lands between crossings and gives no way to know it did.
= power-cut-mid-crossing
m the depth record, the crossing lock and the log
f SIGKILL the machine mid-crossing, lock held, then boot the same disk
e RECOVER: coherent rung, no leaked lock, committed log records intact
t vm/crash

# NOT SYMMETRIC WITH BACKWARD, which is why both are declared. Backward makes
# `now - then` negative and DISABLES a mechanism for the length of the skew;
# forward makes every record look old, so cooldowns expire EARLY and the failure
# mode is a burst. The ceiling half is input-counters.t's (a forward jump is
# indistinguishable from a long sampling gap) and is not restated here.
= clock-jumped-forward
m the supervision timer
f step the guest clock forward an hour under a live ladder
e DEGRADE: no new FAILs, no alert storm, and no false skew diagnosis
t fault-clock

# THE DANGEROUS DIRECTION, AND NOBODY HAD DECLARED IT. Only "forward" was on
# this list, and backward is the one that disables things: every record here is
# stamped with `date +%s` and compared later against `date +%s`, so a backward
# step makes `now - then` NEGATIVE, and a negative satisfies every `-lt <bound>`
# and `-le <bound>` in the package. Three safety mechanisms switched themselves
# off, measured: phantom-guard blocked every idle lock (THE SCREEN NEVER LOCKS),
# _alert_repeat deduped every alert (nobody is told), and _crossing_inflight
# suppressed the standing recheck. All three silently. That is the "a check with
# a hardcoded subject cannot see the thing nobody thought to list" lesson,
# arriving in the list itself.
#
# THE STUB TIER HOLDS THE THREE CONSEQUENCES DOWN (block.t, standing-recheck.t
# 5b and 9b) by planting the record state a backward step produces, and the
# corpus kills a mutation of each of the four guards. What only a REAL step can
# do is exercise all twelve elapsed-time computations at once, with no judgement
# of mine in the loop: I audited twelve and MEASURED three while REASONING about
# nine, and a clock is on this file's own list of things that may never be
# faked.
#
# IT USED TO HANG THE GUEST, and the recorded diagnosis was WRONG. This
# declaration said "the likely cause is that a backward step makes the user
# manager stall". It did not: the scenario passes BYTE-IDENTICAL, three runs in
# a row, with no change to it at all. What changed is the guest: f0dabcc
# stopped the setup's uinput probe suspending the machine that was running the
# test (HandleLidSwitch defaulted to suspend). Same root cause as the lid cells,
# which were also set aside as "cause unknown".
#
# The evidence was unobtainable at the time because the guest buffers its whole
# log and dumps it at the end, so a hang produced NOTHING to read. That is fixed
# structurally rather than remembered: test/vm/run bounds the suite and names
# the scenario it hung in, from a breadcrumb test/run writes.
#
# AND THE ALERT CASE WAS VACUOUS until a mutation said so. It armed its dedup
# stamp AFTER stopping the locker, which crosses `unlock`, so the stamp was for
# a drift at 'open' while the case raises one at 'lock', different key,
# nothing suppressible, passes either way. It arms at the rung under test now
# and asserts the alert that gets through IS the stamped finding.
#
# The one case a real step could never cover either: a future-stamped CROSSING
# MARKER, which exists only DURING a crossing. standing-recheck.t 9b plants it,
# and planting vigilance's own state is honest in the stub tier where faking a
# machine is not.
= clock-jumped-backward
m every elapsed-time computation: a wall clock is not monotonic
f step the guest clock back an hour with records already stamped
e DEGRADE: no mechanism disables itself; a future stamp is not a measurement
t fault-clock

# NOT "ONE CROSSING", and the measurement is why. A hundred requests produced 5
# crossings and 95 that never reached the ladder at all, absorbed at the signal
# layer before `vigilant` runs. The claim is an ORDER OF MAGNITUDE, plus the
# thing a low count cannot distinguish on its own: that the listener is still
# alive when the burst ends.
= lock-signal-storm
m the logind trigger
f a hundred lock-session signals in a burst
e ACT: crossings collapse; the dedup holds; the listener still secures after
t fault-storm

# THE MOST LIKELY OUTSIDE-INTEGRATOR FAILURE, now that the repo is public.
# Everything about the lock leans on a per-user systemd bus: the provider starts
# the locker as a transient --user unit and locker-up asks systemd whether it is
# active. A reader whose stack has no such bus cannot lock, and the only
# question is whether vigilance SAYS SO or reports a locked session.
#
# The bus is really STOPPED (the socket unit), not simulated with a bogus
# DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS, which would be faking a process.
= user-bus-killed
m the lock provider
f stop the user dbus socket so the transient unit cannot be started
e ALERT: the provider fails LOUDLY rather than reporting a lock
t fault-userbus

= backlight-device-removed
m the peripheral actuators
f remove the sysfs backlight between a descent and its ascent
e DEGRADE: the hook goes n/a and drops its stale save
t hooks

# A SLOPE, NOT AN ENDURANCE RUN. "A thousand cycles and see" needs both the
# thousand and a failure big enough to notice; counting the state after N and
# again after M catches a leak of ONE FILE PER CROSSING in forty, in seconds.
# SOAK_CYCLES=1000 still does the long version for an operator.
= soak-a-thousand-cycles
m the ladder
f N lock/unlock cycles, and the SLOPE between two counts (default 40)
e ACT: state flat, no leaked lock, one dedup key, the log still complete
t soak

# TWO ACTUATORS, ONE DEVICE, which is the class that cost twelve hours once:
# mute-leds darkened an LED at `sleep` while the audio driver relit it, and
# vigilance raised 278 drift alerts about a fight it could not win. The rival
# here is REAL: a process driving the same compositor sway-dpms drives.
#
# AN X11 RIVAL IS NOW POSSIBLE TOO, and this note used to say it was not. The
# reason given was that the guest's X servers had no DPMS extension and `xset s`
# was silently ignored, so an X-side rival would have no device to contend for
# and the cell would pass vacuously. That was true of Xvfb and stopped being
# true when the guest gained a real Xorg over DRM (the `x11dpms` capability):
# DPMS comes from the DRIVER, not the server, and modesetting on /dev/dri/card0
# provides it. `xfce4-power-manager` in the xfce flavour is exactly that rival,
# and it needs the flavour because it will not start without a notification
# service. Left as its own cell rather than folded in here: a second actuator on
# a DIFFERENT mechanism is a different claim, not a bigger version of this one.
= competing-actuator
m the peripheral actuators and the standing recheck
f a second process re-asserts `dpms on` while the rung is dark
e ALERT: the drift is named once, the edge still crosses, and it clears
t fault-actuator

# THE SAME COEXISTENCE FAULT ON THE EDGE THAT MATTERS MOST. Above, a rival
# drives a PERIPHERAL and the worst case is a lit panel. Here a rival drives the
# LOCK, and the worst case is the record reading `open` with the screen covered,
# which every tier that trusts the record then agrees with.
#
# IT IS THE OUTSIDE INTEGRATOR'S CASE, not ours. tackup's config test already
# asserts OUR keybind crosses the edge rather than calling a locker directly,
# and that guard cannot reach a third party: xfce4-session's Recommends alone
# pull light-locker, xfce4-screensaver, xscreensaver and mate-screensaver, and
# xss-lock plus i3lock is the same rival assembled by hand.
#
# THE RESPONSE IS DELIBERATELY SPLIT, because the two halves have different
# standing. RECOVER is certain and asserted: whatever vigilance can see, a rival
# that comes and goes must not leave the ladder unable to secure the session.
# Whether the desync itself can be NOTICED depends on evidence the package may
# not have, so the scenario prints every candidate signal and the verdict is
# recorded from that measurement rather than asserted from reasoning.
= rival-locker
m locker-up, report's coherence section, and the depth record
f a REAL foreign locker (i3lock on :98) holds the screen with no edge crossed
e RECOVER: the ladder still locks afterwards; detection is MEASURED, see below
t fault-rival-locker
